    snippet: { lang: "python", code: "    def _ordered_moves(self, board: chess.Board, ply: int, tt_move: chess.Move | None) -> tuple[list[chess.Move], float]:\n        moves = list(board.legal_moves)\n        if not moves:\n            return [], 0.0\n        priors, value = self._priors_and_value(board, moves)\n        order = np.argsort(-priors)[: self._width(ply)]\n        chosen = [moves[i] for i in order]\n        if tt_move is not None and tt_move in moves:\n            # The best move from a previous (shallower) iteration is the\n            # single most valuable ordering hint there is -- search it\n            # first even if this iteration's priors would have pruned it.\n            if tt_move in chosen:\n                chosen.remove(tt_move)\n            chosen.insert(0, tt_move)\n        return chosen, value", note: "src/chessml/search.py, lines 222-236 (verified verbatim): the policy head's softmax over legal moves orders the candidates and only the top `width(ply)` (default 10/6/4/3/2) are searched, with the transposition-table move promoted to the front." },
